  "notes": "The lane's most Prince-indebted male voice, and the entry names a deliberate contradiction to model: \"a deliberately mechanical feel that contrasts with the harmonic looseness\". So this model carries the highest `quantizeStrength` and the lowest `velocityVar` in the section — a drum-machine kit with the snare exactly on the grid and a clap layer, quiet 16th hats, and a live-percussion overlay on top — under harmony described as \"m9 and maj7#5 colours, unexpected borrowed chords mid-phrase, and modulation into the bridge — harmonically busier than the radio-R&B norm of the same years\", which is the raised ninth weight and `susSubstitutionProb`. The band is the entry's 74–104. The melodic parameter is stated exactly: \"falsetto with a wide leap on the hook — the top line jumps a sixth or octave into the chorus, then descends in small steps, with dense self-harmonised stacks behind it\", which is `octaveJumpProb` 0.28, a leap share of 0.12, a descent-weighted contour and an `octave_echo` counter. Bass is \"synth bass with a plucked attack and short decay, root-and-octave\", so it mirrors the kick at a high root probability. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every drum number, the melody figures and the harmonic weights; the research marks the whole parametric detail as lane inference and rates the entry medium. ⚠ No keys are authored: the entry names none, only a mode (\"minor-mode with chromatic keyboard motion\"), so the lane's keys are inherited.",
